 ~120 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Shedeur Sanders</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 JAX allow <strong>252 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 JAX concede <strong>68 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58). A rec-yards spot.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>KC Concepcion</strong> (WR2) \u2014 JAX leak 44 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 JAX are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.31 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone CLE throw it (56% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Low-total grind</strong> \u2014 a 40 total signals a slower, run-leaning game; skew toward unders, especially on secondary pass-catchers.</p><h3>JAX \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>JAX are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Chris Rodriguez Jr.</strong> (11 carries, 50 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though JAX's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Parker Washington</strong> projects for <strong>7 targets and 61 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Brenton Strange</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 35 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>CLE \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>CLE are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 CLE project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Quinshon Judkins</strong>'s rushing projection (12 carries, 45 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Jerry Jeudy</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, CLE funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>5 targets for 46 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Harold Fannin Jr.</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 44 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 8-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"CLE","col":"#FF7A45","players":[{"name":"Shedeur Sanders","pos":"QB","starter":false,"minor":true,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":196.87,"book":null,"over_th":183.34,"under_th":205.19,"call":null,"edge":null},{"